---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/food-science/foundational/food-chemistry-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Food science begins with a chemical re-framing: a meal is a matrix of water, carbohydrates,
    proteins and lipids — and its shelf life, texture and taste all follow from how those molecules
    interact. The single most useful number in the discipline is not how much water food contains
    but how <strong>available</strong> that water is: water activity, a-w.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reframing matters because everyday intuitions about food are chemical misconceptions.
    "Fresh" food is not food without chemistry — it is chemistry in its most active phase:
    enzymes still working after harvest (the apple browning, the banana ripening), reactions
    running between sugars and proteins, oxygen quietly attacking fats. "Preservatives" are
    not necessarily synthetic additives — salt, sugar, smoke, acid, cold and drying are the
    original preservative toolkit, and every one works by a chemical or physical mechanism this
    chapter will name. And "texture", which most people think of as physics, is polymer
    science: bread crumb, custard gel and chocolate snap are three states of matter that
    macromolecules make. Food chemistry is the discipline of controlling reactions we usually
    experience only as their results.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with the master variable that organises the whole pantry: water.
  </p>

  <h2>Water activity: the master variable</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="water activity, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={awStepsCfg} />
  <p>
    The definition deserves one more pass, because it is counterintuitive.
    <strong>Water activity</strong> (a<sub>w</sub>) is the food's equilibrium relative
    humidity, expressed as a fraction from 0 (bone dry) to 1.0 (pure water): put the food in
    a sealed box and a<sub>w</sub> is the humidity the air settles at. It measures how
    tightly the food's matrix holds its water — water bound to sugars, salts and protein
    surfaces is present but "unavailable", unable to dissolve nutrients, swell proteins or
    cross a microbial membrane. That is why two foods with the same water content can behave
    completely differently: fresh meat (~70% water) spoils in days because its water is
    nearly free (a<sub>w</sub> ≈ 0.99), while jam at ~30% water keeps for years because the
    sugar locks most of it (a<sub>w</sub> ≈ 0.75–0.85). Honey (a<sub>w</sub> ≈ 0.5–0.6) is
    the extreme: archaeologically edible, microbially sterile.
  </p>
  <p>
    The microbial thresholds in the widget are the practical payoff. Most spoilage bacteria
    need a<sub>w</sub> above about <strong>0.91</strong>; yeasts tolerate down to ~0.88;
    moulds down to ~0.80; the famous extremophile xerophilic moulds go a little lower still.
    Below a microbe's threshold it does not die — it simply cannot grow, which is why
    lowering a<sub>w</sub> preserves without sterilising. Salt, sugar and drying all work the
    same way: they bind water and drop the a<sub>w</sub>. Jam, jerky, honey, salted cod,
    Christmas pudding and parmesan are one idea — a<sub>w</sub> control — at different
    sweetnesses and saltinesses. Safety thresholds follow the same logic (the toxin-forming
    <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> stops below ~0.86), which is why water activity sits in
    food-safety regulations worldwide.
  </p>
  <p>
    But chemistry has its own curve. <strong>Lipid oxidation</strong> — the attack of oxygen
    on unsaturated fats, producing the stale, cardboard rancidity of old crisps and nuts —
    accelerates at the <em>dry</em> end, where surface fats sit exposed with little water to
    shield them. Microbial spoilage rises as water becomes available, peaking near a-w of 0.9 and collapsing
    below the microbial thresholds. The two curves cross, and every preserved
    food lives in the gap between them: dry enough to stop microbes, not so dry (or stored so
    long) that oxidation wins. Add the third shelf-life reaction — <em>enzymatic browning</em>,
    which needs water to run — and "shelf life" becomes a multi-front war with one main
    weapon: knowing each reaction's a<sub>w</sub> turf.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="why jam keeps but crackers go rancid" widget="plotter" config={awCfg} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "no water means no spoilage" and "sugar is just for
  sweetness". Dry foods still oxidise (curve two) and staling involves water <em>movement</em>,
  not just quantity; and in most preserved foods sugar's primary job is colligative —
  binding water — with sweetness a side effect. A jam recipe is a water-activity
  prescription that happens to taste good.</p>

  <h2>The macronutrients as materials</h2>
  <p>
    Carbohydrates, proteins and lipids are not just nutrition here — they are structural materials.
    Starch granules gelatinise and retrograde (why bread stales), proteins unfold and knit into
    networks (why egg sets and yoghurt gels), and fats crystallise into polymorphs (why chocolate
    snaps or blooms). Texture is applied polymer science.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take each mechanism in turn. <strong>Starch</strong> is packed in granules as semi-crystalline
    lumps; heated in water above about 60–70 °C the crystals melt, water floods in, and the
    granules swell to many times their volume — <em>gelatinisation</em>, the difference
    between flour in sauce and a sauce. On cooling and ageing, the dissolved starch chains
    re-associate into ordered, water-shedding crystallites — <em>retrogradation</em> — which
    is the physical chemistry of staling: bread does not go stale by drying (reheating
    retrograded bread re-melts the crystallites and "refreshes" it), it goes stale by
    recrystallising. <strong>Proteins</strong> are chains folded into precise shapes
    (see the biochemistry unit for the mechanism); heat, acid or beating unfolds them
    (<em>denaturation</em>), exposing sticky regions that bond to their neighbours — and when
    the neighbours form a continuous mesh, that is a gel: the set of a boiled egg, the body
    of yoghurt, the collapse-proof network of a soufflé. <strong>Gluten</strong> is the same
    physics with kneading instead of heat: wheat proteins crosslink into an elastic network
    that traps the gas of fermentation — bread's entire architecture. <strong>Lipids</strong>
    solidify into crystals, and their triglycerides pack in several distinct crystalline
    forms — <em>polymorphs</em> — with different melting points and shapes; cocoa butter is
    the famous case, where only one polymorph (form V) gives the glossy snap of proper
    chocolate, and the wrong one gives the grey, crumbly <em>bloom</em> of a badly stored bar
    (tempering is polymorph control). Fats also <em>emulsify</em> and carry texture — mayonnaise is oil droplets held apart by egg-protein membranes — and their melting
    profile over body temperature is why butter, unlike most vegetable oils, "melts" on the
    tongue.
  </p>

  <h2>Flavour is chemistry too</h2>
  <p>
    Taste is receptor chemistry (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami), but much of what we call
    flavour arrives retronasally from volatile molecules — and many of the best ones are reaction
    products. The Maillard reaction between reducing sugars and amino acids builds hundreds of
    aroma compounds; it is why toast, roast coffee and seared steak share a family resemblance.
    Control the chemistry, control the craving.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mechanism is worth knowing because it explains the craft. The <strong>Maillard
    reaction</strong> begins when a reducing sugar's carbonyl group attacks an amino group
    (from an amino acid or protein side chain), and cascades through rearrangements and
    fragmentations into hundreds of final products: pyrazines (roasted, nutty), furans
    (caramel-like), sulfur compounds (meaty, coffee-like — small amounts, enormous impact).
    It runs fastest at intermediate moisture, needs heat, and browns as it flavours — one
    reaction, simultaneously producing the colour, the crust and the aroma of a sear.
    <strong>Caramelisation</strong> is its cousin without amino acids: sugar alone heated past
    its melting point decomposes into buttery, bitter, nutty volatiles. Temperature and pH
    are the cooks' control knobs — a steak at 150 °C surface temperature Maillards in
    seconds; a boiled (100 °C) steak never browns, which is the whole reason for drying the
    surface and using a screaming-hot pan. Umami rounds out the receptor half of the story:
    glutamate plus the nucleotides inosinate and guanylate act <em>synergistically</em> —
    kombu (glutamate) plus bonito (inosinate) is the classical pairing, and the combination
    tastes far stronger than the sum, which is why stock-making is a chemistry of pairing.
    Bitterness, sweetness and salt interact at the receptor too — a pinch of salt in caramel
    or chocolate suppresses bitterness and amplifies sweetness, a perceptual mechanism food
    designers use as deliberately as any ingredient.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three frameworks carry food chemistry into professional territory. First, the
    <strong>glass transition</strong>: below a temperature-and-moisture-dependent point, the
    amorphous parts of a food (starch, sugar matrices) become glassy — brittle, diffusion-
    almost-stopped, maximally stable; most "shelf-stable" dry foods are engineered to stay
    below their glass transition, and every softening, caking or stickiness failure is the
    material crossing above it (the powder that turned into a brick in the bag). Water
    activity and glass transition are the two axes of the modern stability map, and Labuza's
    classic curves were their first sketch. Second, <strong>hurdle technology</strong>: real
    products rarely rely on one preservation factor but stack sub-lethal "hurdles" — low
    pH plus low a<sub>w</sub> plus preservative plus refrigeration — so that no microbe can
    clear all of them at once; chorizo (fermentation acid + salt + drying + spice
    antimicrobials) is the textbook example, and the design principle generalises to any
    reformulation challenge (cut the salt, add another hurdle). Third, the
    <strong>frontier of flavour chemistry</strong>: gas-chromatography–mass-spectrometry plus
    human sniffing (GC–O) can now identify the dozen or so "odorants that matter" in a food
    out of hundreds present — tomato volatiles are largely supplied by C6 aldehydes cleaved
    from membrane lipids in seconds after cutting, which is why a chilled tomato tastes
    dull: the enzyme's membrane fluidity dropped and the aroma factory stalled. Refrigeration,
    ripening chemistry and flavour design meet in one measurement — the field's habit of
    converting a cooking intuition (serve tomatoes at room temperature) into a molecular
    mechanism is exactly what this unit's fundamentals are for.
  </p>
  <p>
    One worked product development shows the fundamentals integrating, because that is how
    the discipline is actually used. Task: design a shelf-stable, soft-baked fruit bar.
    <em>Water activity first</em>: the target is below ~0.85 (mould floor) but above the
    point where the texture turns leathery and the sugars begin to glass; the recipe
    balances fruit purée's free water against added sugar (binds water, lowers
    a<sub>w</sub>) and pectin (binds water into a gel network that holds the water it can).
    <em>Macronutrient engineering:</em> the purée's pectin sets the gel (a polysaccharide
    network), any added milk-protein powder adds chew and nutrition but also Maillard
    reactivity with the fruit's reducing sugars during baking — so baking temperature and
    time are chosen as much for colour control as for moisture loss. <em>Lipid
    considerations:</em> nuts add fat, which adds rancidity risk — the dry-ish, oxygen-
    exposed bar sits on the wrong side of the oxidation curve, so vitamin E (an antioxidant)
    or nitrogen-flushed packaging earns its place. <em>Flavour chemistry:</em> the bake is a
    controlled Maillard event — enough for toasty depth, not so much that the fruity esters
    are overpowered; a squeeze of acid keeps pH low, which both favours pectin gelation and
    adds a preservative hurdle. Notice the pattern: every design decision is one of this
    chapter's mechanisms with a number attached — a<sub>w</sub> target, gelation chemistry,
    a polyphenol or antioxidant choice, a Maillard window. That is food chemistry's working
    definition: the fundamentals are not background reading; they are the parameter list.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IFT undergraduate education standards; Belitz, Grosch &amp; Schieberle, <em>Food Chemistry</em>; Labuza's classic work on water activity and shelf life; McGee, <em>On Food and Cooking</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
